; ===========================================================================
; routines.src — motion subprograms for the pick-and-place cell.
;
; *** NOT VERIFIED AGAINST HARDWARE. ***
; This project has no KUKA controller, no KRL compiler and no
; OrangeEdit/WorkVisual instance. Nothing in this file has been compiled,
; loaded or executed. It is written against KUKA's published manuals and
; every construct that is not plain arithmetic carries the citation it
; rests on; a citation is evidence that the manual says so, not evidence
; that the controller accepted it. Read it as a design artefact.
;
; Sources cited below:
;   [EXP]    KUKA, Expert Programming — KSS 5.2, ProgExperteBHR5.2
;   [SYSVAR] KUKA, System Variables — KSS, SysVar 08.02.03
; Both are older KSS releases than KSS 8.x; every construct cited is core
; KRL that is unchanged in 8.x. See
; docs/superpowers/audit/2026-08-14-T-kuka-conformance.md for the
; provenance of each quotation.
;
; The receive loop that calls these subprograms is laptop_comm.src.
; Commands arrive as 16-byte binary frames (../protocol.py) on the
; EthernetKRL channel configured in laptop-comm.xml.
;
; Two structural rules this file used to break, both fixed 2026-08-14:
;
;   File naming [EXP §"Programs and files"]: "The object name without an
;   extension is also the name of the file and is therefore prefixed by
;   DEF." The first DEF in this file must therefore be routines(). It
;   used to be DEF MoveBetweenPositions(...), which the editor rejects on
;   load.
;
;   GLOBAL [EXP §6]: "Local subprograms/functions can only be called from
;   within the SRC file in which they were programmed. ... Alternatively,
;   a local subprogram can be preceded by the keyword GLOBAL."
;   laptop_comm.src is a different SRC file, so everything it calls here
;   is GLOBAL. None of these carried GLOBAL before, so none was reachable.
;
; A file taken off a real controller also carries &ACCESS / &REL / &PARAM
; attribute lines written by the editor. They are omitted deliberately:
; this file has never been on a controller and should not look as if it
; had.
; ===========================================================================


DEF routines()
   ; Entry point that exists to satisfy the file-naming rule. The cell's
   ; real entry point is laptop_comm.src. Selecting this file by accident
   ; therefore initialises motion and returns rather than moving the arm.
   InitMotion()
END


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; InitMotion — must run before the first PTP of any program in this cell.
;
; [EXP §"PTP motions"]: "$VEL_AXIS[axis number]: to program maximum
; axis-specific velocities, and $ACC_AXIS[axis number]: to program
; maximum axis-specific acceleration rates. All entries are given as
; percentages of the maximum value defined in the machine data. If these
; two system variables have not been programmed for all axes, execution
; of the program will cause a corresponding error message to be
; generated."
;
; Nothing in this file used to program them, so the first PTP would have
; faulted at run time. BAS(#INITMOV,0) is the KUKA basis-package call
; that programs $VEL_AXIS[], $ACC_AXIS[], $VEL and $ACC from the machine
; data; it is the call the controller's own program template places in
; its BASISTECH INI fold, and it appears in KUKA's EthernetKRL manual in
; the BinaryFixed sample program. BAS is declared globally in
; $CONFIG.DAT on every KRC, so no EXT declaration is needed here.
;
; NOT VERIFIED, and not verifiable here: TOOL_DATA[1] and BASE_DATA[1]
; are the taught tool and base of a cell that does not exist. Selecting
; index 1 is a convention this file states; it is not a measurement.
; A production INI fold additionally arms the standard stop-message
; interrupt (GLOBAL INTERRUPT DECL 3 WHEN $STOPMESS==TRUE DO IR_STOPM()).
; It is left out because nothing here can test it, not because a real
; cell should omit it.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GLOBAL DEF InitMotion()
   BAS(#INITMOV, 0)

   $TOOL = TOOL_DATA[1]
   $BASE = BASE_DATA[1]
   $IPO_MODE = #BASE
END


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TargetFromXYZ — build a fully-defined E6POS from three wire coordinates.
;
; THE ORIENTATION DECISION. The 16-byte frame carries X, Y and Z and
; nothing else (../protocol.py). A, B, C, the configuration bits S and T,
; and the external-axis components E1..E6 are not commanded and cannot
; be: there are no fields for them. Something must supply them. Until
; 2026-08-14 nothing did — SimpleMove's comment claimed "we copy the
; current orientation" and no line of code copied anything, so every
; PTP and LIN in this file was issued to an E6POS whose orientation and
; configuration were never assigned.
;
; Decided: DERIVE FROM THE CURRENT POSE. The target starts as a whole-
; structure copy of $POS_ACT — "current robot position" of type E6POS
; [SYSVAR] — and only X, Y and Z are overwritten. Copying the whole
; structure rather than five named components is deliberate: it leaves no
; component of the E6POS unassigned, including E1..E6.
;
; What that buys and what it costs, stated rather than left to be found:
;   * The wire commands translation only. Orientation is whatever the arm
;     already holds, so the cell's start-up procedure — not this program
;     — establishes the tool attitude. There is no fixed tool-down A/B/C
;     constant here because it would have to be invented; this repository
;     holds no taught cell data.
;   * Carrying S and T forward holds the arm configuration constant
;     across a PTP, which is what a fixed-tool pick-and-place wants: the
;     arm will not flip elbow or wrist mid-cycle. A target reachable only
;     in a different configuration is then refused rather than silently
;     reconfigured, and a refusal is the correct outcome.
;   * Reading a $-variable stops the advance run [EXP §5.1: "all
;     input/output instructions and access to inputs/outputs via system
;     variables trigger an advance run stop"], so this costs
;     continuous-path smoothness at every target built. For a
;     point-to-point cycle that is an acceptable price; for a contouring
;     application it would not be.
;
; The alternative — a fixed tool-down orientation — is the better choice
; on a commissioned cell, and it needs exactly one measurement that this
; project does not have.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GLOBAL DEF TargetFromXYZ(target :OUT, x_mm :IN, y_mm :IN, z_mm :IN)
   DECL E6POS target
   DECL REAL x_mm
   DECL REAL y_mm
   DECL REAL z_mm

   target = $POS_ACT
   target.X = x_mm
   target.Y = y_mm
   target.Z = z_mm
END


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; MoveBetweenPositions — a two-point shuttle.
;
; DEAD CODE, and labelled as such rather than quietly kept: no opcode in
; execution/protocol.py's OpCode enum maps to it and laptop_comm.src
; never calls it. It is retained only because it is the smallest readable
; example of the calling convention this file uses, and it is corrected
; alongside the rest so that it does not sit here as the one subprogram
; that would still fail to load.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GLOBAL DEF MoveBetweenPositions(pos1 :IN, pos2 :IN)
   DECL E6POS pos1
   DECL E6POS pos2

   PTP pos1
   WAIT SEC 1

   PTP pos2
   WAIT SEC 1
END


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; SimpleMove — the motion behind OpCode.MOVE_TO.
;
;   pos          : commanded Cartesian target. Only X/Y/Z are commanded;
;                  the caller is expected to have built it with
;                  TargetFromXYZ, which supplies the rest.
;   ptp_vel_pct  : PTP velocity, PER CENT of the machine-data maximum
;   ptp_acc_pct  : PTP acceleration, PER CENT of the machine-data maximum
;   wait_time    : dwell after the move, seconds
;
; The velocity parameters used to be written to $VEL.CP and $ACC.CP and
; then handed to a PTP, where they govern nothing: [EXP §"PTP motions"]
; names $VEL_AXIS[] and $ACC_AXIS[] as the variables a PTP obeys, and
; [SYSVAR] gives $VEL.CP as the CP velocity in m/s, which applies to LIN
; and CIRC. The parameters were therefore inert — the entire purpose of
; the subprogram. They now carry percentages and are written to the
; arrays the motion actually reads, so the unit of the parameter changed
; with its meaning. Nothing on the wire sets them; laptop_comm.src passes
; the cell defaults.
;
; Six axes, because the KR 6 R700 sixx has six and this cell has no
; external axes. A cell with external axes must also program
; $VEL_EXTAX[] / $ACC_EXTAX[].
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GLOBAL DEF SimpleMove(pos :IN, ptp_vel_pct :IN, ptp_acc_pct :IN, wait_time :IN)
   DECL E6POS pos
   DECL INT ptp_vel_pct
   DECL INT ptp_acc_pct
   DECL REAL wait_time
   DECL INT i

   FOR i = 1 TO 6
      $VEL_AXIS[i] = ptp_vel_pct
      $ACC_AXIS[i] = ptp_acc_pct
   ENDFOR

   PTP pos
   WAIT SEC wait_time
END


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; VacuumControl — vacuum gripper, driven by OpCode.VACUUM_ON / VACUUM_OFF.
;   digital_out  : digital channel for on/off
;   analog_out   : analog channel for the vacuum level, index 1..16
;   vacuum_on    : FALSE = off, TRUE = on
;   vacuum_level : 0..100 percentage
;
; THE SCALING. [SYSVAR] gives $ANOUT[n] as: data type REAL, minimum
; -1.0, maximum +1.0, unit V, "[n] = [1] ... [16]", "-1.0 -> -10 V",
; "+1.0 -> +10 V". This subprogram used to assign the 0..100 percentage
; straight into it. That compiles — KRL widens INT to REAL — and then
; silently collapses the whole scale: every value from 1 to 100 is out of
; range, is clamped to full scale, and raises a controller message, so a
; vacuum_pct of 40 and one of 95 are the same +10 V at the gripper. The
; percentage the Python side threads through aux_u16 did nothing.
;
; Divided by 100.0 the percentage maps onto 0.0..1.0, i.e. 0..+10 V. The
; clamp is here so that a host-side bug cannot reach the annunciated
; out-of-range path at all: a percentage outside 0..100 is a fault in the
; caller, and saturating it here is the conservative reading — it cannot
; command more suction than full scale, and it cannot command negative
; voltage on a channel that means suction.
;
; NOT VERIFIED: that 0..10 V linearly means 0..100 % vacuum is a property
; of the ejector, not of the controller. No datasheet for the gripper
; exists in this repository.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GLOBAL DEF VacuumControl(digital_out :IN, analog_out :IN, vacuum_on :IN, vacuum_level :IN)
   DECL INT digital_out
   DECL INT analog_out
   DECL BOOL vacuum_on
   DECL INT vacuum_level
   DECL INT pct

   pct = vacuum_level
   IF pct < 0 THEN
      pct = 0
   ENDIF
   IF pct > 100 THEN
      pct = 100
   ENDIF

   $OUT[digital_out] = vacuum_on
   $ANOUT[analog_out] = pct / 100.0
END


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; PickAndPlace — the canonical cycle, OpCode.PICK_AND_PLACE.
;   pick_pos   : Cartesian pose of the battery to grasp
;   place_pos  : Cartesian pose of the cell to place into
;   vacuum_pct : gripper vacuum level, 0..100
;
; Returns a common.types.RobotStatusCode value. tests/test_execution.py
; pins every `RETURN n ; NAME` line below against that enum.
;
; DEFFCT, not DEF. [EXP §6]: "Unlike a subprogram, a function sends back
; a return value. A function begins with the keyword DEFFCT. The data
; type of the return value is specified directly after the keyword
; DEFFCT. The return value itself is transferred via RETURN. The function
; is terminated using the keyword ENDFCT." This was `DEF PickAndPlace(...)
; ... RETURN 2 ... END`: in a subprogram RETURN takes no operand, it only
; exits, so supplying one is an inadmissible instruction and the file
; would not compile. The caller must now consume the value —
; laptop_comm.src does, as `status = PickAndPlace(...)`.
;
; Sequence (PPR §5.4):
;   approach > grasp > transport > insert > release > retract
;
; The insert depth is the Z of place_pos, which laptop_comm.src takes
; from the latched place pose, not from the wire: the frame has one Z
; field and the pick needs it. See KukaClient.pick_and_place.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GLOBAL DEFFCT INT PickAndPlace(pick_pos :IN, place_pos :IN, vacuum_pct :IN)
   DECL E6POS pick_pos
   DECL E6POS place_pos
   DECL INT vacuum_pct
   DECL E6POS approach_pos
   DECL E6POS transport_pos

   ; [SYSVAR] $VEL: "Velocities in the advance run. CP = m/s"; $ACC:
   ; "Accelerations in the advance run. CP = m/s^2". So 0.150 genuinely
   ; is 150 mm/s, comfortably under the 250 mm/s reduced-velocity limit
   ; that applies in T1, and 0.500 m/s^2 reaches it in 0.3 s over 22 mm.
   ; These govern the LIN segments below. They do NOT govern the two PTP
   ; segments — those obey $VEL_AXIS[]/$ACC_AXIS[], programmed by
   ; InitMotion via BAS(#INITMOV,0) — which is why the FDR's "the only
   ; real cap is $VEL.CP" needed narrowing.
   $VEL.CP = 0.150        ; 150 mm/s default — within the R5 safety cap
   $ACC.CP = 0.500

   ; 1. Approach — 60 mm above the target
   approach_pos = pick_pos
   approach_pos.Z = pick_pos.Z + 60
   PTP approach_pos
   LIN pick_pos

   ; 2. Grasp
   VacuumControl(1, 1, TRUE, vacuum_pct)
   WAIT SEC 0.05
   ; [EXP §5.1] "all input/output instructions and access to inputs/
   ; outputs via system variables trigger an advance run stop", so this
   ; reads the sensor in the main run and needs no CONTINUE.
   IF $IN[10] == FALSE THEN    ; vacuum pressure sensor
      RETURN 2                 ; PICK_FAILED
   ENDIF

   ; 3. Transport — retract then move to place XY at safe height
   LIN approach_pos
   transport_pos = place_pos
   transport_pos.Z = place_pos.Z + 80
   PTP transport_pos

   ; 4. Insert (LIN for controlled descent)
   LIN place_pos

   ; 5. Release
   VacuumControl(1, 1, FALSE, 0)
   WAIT SEC 0.03

   ; 6. Retract
   LIN transport_pos

   ; There is no PLACE_FAILED branch here and there is no sensor that
   ; could produce one: nothing on this arm reports that a cell seated.
   ; execution/mock_kuka_server.py does emit PLACE_FAILED (3), which is a
   ; simulator behaviour with no controller-side counterpart.
   RETURN 1                    ; SUCCESS
ENDFCT
